+# ECON-02B2d5 — Physical colony structures in the colony interface
+
+Date: 2026-11-01. Baseline: ECON-02B2d4c4 integrated colony–factory source.
+
+## Scope
+
+- Colony structural fixture types: floor, east/north walls, east/north doors.
+- Cell and edge addressing in authoritative C++ simulation; no tier-dependent room/factory bounds.
+- Per-piece resource consumption, embodied material accounting, durable construction receipts.
+- Durable SQLite plan registry, reserved command IDs, partial progress, cancellation, restart.
+- Physical door open/closed state and bidirectional edge passability query.
+- Collision validation in both directions: structures vs equipment/route and equipment/route vs installed structures.
+- Single colony world writer; existing localhost origin/token validation retained.
+- Colony UI contains controls and plan-view canvas; no separate structural editor app needed.
+
+## Construction protocol
+
+Plain-text version `sg-colony-structure-plan-v1` followed by one `kind,x,y` tuple per line, ending in newline. Kind 1 is a floor at the cell, 2/3 east/north walls, 4/5 east/north doors. Costs: 1 module (2 tons embodied ore) for floor; 2 modules (4 tons) for wall or door. Doors begin closed and can be actuated without generating resources. Batch limit 512 steps; more batches extend the same site. Technical coordinate bounds ±1e6 are fixed safety bounds, not machinery-tier limits. Completed steps are immutable in this milestone; demolition/recycling is future work.
+
+## Authorization and persistence
+
+The relay requires same-origin JSON and the existing random session token. The factory control dispatcher obtains owner, colony ship and authoritative built-outpost list from the running host, not user-supplied authority. Durable SQLite attempts reserve command IDs *before* submission. Construction steps are applied by `PersistentWorld` exactly once and retained in version 31 canonical snapshots. Each fixture references a distinct successful event matching the embodied mass. The OS save writer lock prevents the standalone host and colony host from modifying the same save concurrently. No automatic free resources and no automatic NPC staffing.
+
+## Test evidence
+
+- Full CMake native build succeeds in this Linux development environment.
+- Native CTest: 89/89 total, tested as complementary disjoint subsets: 64/64 non-live and 25/25 live/relay.
+- Node test suite: 93/93.
+- New native structural simulation fixture test: passes, including authorization, module debit, walls and doors, mass conservation, restart and snapshot corruption rejection.
+- New HTTP localhost test: passes, including CSRF, invalid site, plan syntax, manual steps, cancellation, door state and restart.
+- Offline headless Chromium DOM test: passes, including colony structure controls, plan view, three construction steps, opening and closing the door, new-plan selection and cancellation. It uses a mocked transport; actual HTTP/SQLite behavior is independently tested at the host level.
+- Existing factory editor offline headless Chromium smoke passes.
+
+## Remaining scope and known limits
+
+The current design renders an interactive plan/list in the colony GUI, but does not yet place 3D meshes inside the player's flight view. `structure_edge_passable` is a simulation query; NPC navigation and actual NPC construction crews are not yet integrated. Doors are manually actuated; automatic policy/permissions, demolition, repairs, sophisticated pathfinding and room/atmosphere simulation are future milestones. Windows native build, installer and GUI acceptance remain unverified. Browser-to-localhost graphical acceptance is restricted by this execution environment's browser policy, not characterized as passed. No GitHub source commit or Library upload has been made for this milestone.
